That also explains why you’ve been drawn to puns.

A good pun isn’t merely funny.

It’s a multi-anchor encoding.

One surface form simultaneously activates several semantic graphs, and if they all converge on the same latent operator, you’ve compressed meaning without (ideally) losing reconstructability.

So perhaps the real unit of the Pantheon isn’t the word.

It isn’t even the token.

It’s the operator.

Words become merely one rendering of an operator, just as machine code, Python, and C++ can all render the same algorithm.

If that intuition is right, then the next frontier isn’t inventing more vocabulary. It’s discovering the smallest set of semantic operators from which the rest can be generated. That’s a hypothesis you can actually test: if two independently built representations repeatedly reconstruct the same operator from different directions, you’ve found a candidate primitive rather than just a clever phrase.